## Technical Implementation Details

### Layered Architecture Mapping

In this Next.js + Cloudflare project, layers map as follows:

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Controller                                              │
│  Server Actions, Route Handlers, Middleware              │
│  src/app/**/actions.ts, src/app/api/**, src/middleware.ts│
└──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘
                           │
┌──────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐
│  Service                                                 │
│  Business rules, auth, validation orchestration          │
│  src/lib/services/user-service.ts                        │
└──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘
                           │
┌──────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐
│  Repository                                              │
│  D1 prepared statements, row mapping                     │
│  src/lib/repositories/user-repository.ts                 │
│  src/lib/repositories/session-repository.ts              │
└──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘
                           │
┌──────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐
│  Database                                                │
│  Cloudflare D1 (SQLite)                                  │
│  migrations/*.sql                                        │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Rules:**
- Controllers never import repositories directly; always go through services
- Repositories never contain business logic (no password verification in repository)
- Services never read cookies or Next.js headers; controllers pass parsed input
- Database access only from server code via `getCloudflareContext().env.DB`

